{
  "raw": {
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/152854": {
      "title": "Berthiaume Family Professor of Neuroscience at the University of Notre Dame",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Science Location: University of Notre Dame, College of Science The University of Notre Dame invites distinguished scholars to apply for the Berthiaume Family Professorship in Neuroscience , an endowed faculty position within the College of Science. This prestigious chair offers a unique opportunity for an exceptional candidate to lead and expand neuroscience research and education at one of the nation's top-tier research universities. As the Berthiaume Family Professor of Neuroscience , you will: Lead a world-class research program focused on innovative and impactful neuroscience research addressing fundamental questions in cellular, molecular, or computational neuroscience. Secure and manage external funding to support cutting-edge research initiatives and collaborate with interdisciplinary teams within and beyond the university. Collaborative Environment: Join a vibrant, interdisciplinary community within the College of Science, with access to state-of-the-art facilities and numerous Centers and Institutes . Opportunities for collaboration with the NIH-funded Indiana Clinical and Translational Sciences Institute (CTSI) are also available. Institutional Support: The University of Notre Dame is an R1 research institution and a member of the AAU , offering access to cutting-edge research facilities. Mentor and guide undergraduate, graduate, and postdoctoral students, fostering a diverse and inclusive research environment that supports the next generation of neuroscientists. Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in neuroscience and related disciplines, demonstrating a commitment to educational excellence and innovation. Engage in service to the department, college, university, and the broader scientific community, contributing to the academic and intellectual life of Notre Dame. About the University of Notre Dame: The University of Notre Dame, a premier R1 research institution in the AAU, is renowned for its commitment to excellence in research, teaching, and societal impact. Located in South Bend, Indiana, our university offers a dynamic academic environment and unparalleled resources for research and education. The College of Science at Notre Dame fosters a culture of discovery, innovation, and inclusivity, supported by state-of-the-art facilities and a collaborative research community. Key Qualifications: Research Excellence: A distinguished record of groundbreaking research in neuroscience, as evidenced by high-impact publications, significant grant funding, and leadership in the field. Demonstrated Leadership: Proven leadership in advancing the field of neuroscience, including leading collaborative research initiatives, serving in leadership roles in professional organizations, journals or academic societies, and contributing to the strategic direction of the field. Teaching and Mentorship: A strong commitment to mentoring and teaching excellence, with extensive experience in developing and delivering courses at both undergraduate and graduate levels. Collaborative Leadership: Proven ability to build and lead interdisciplinary research teams, fostering collaborations across departments and disciplines. Visionary Thinking: A forward-looking approach to neuroscience that integrates cutting-edge research with societal needs, addressing critical challenges in health, behavior, and technology. Service: Experience contributing to the academic and professional community through service roles, including participation in committees, editorial boards, and professional organizations. Required Qualifications: PhD, MD, or equivalent terminal degree in Neuroscience or a related field A distinguished record of research, teaching, and service that qualifies for appointment in an endowed professorship at the rank of Full Professor with tenure. Demonstrated leadership in neuroscience research and education, with a strong commitment to advancing the mission of the University of Notre Dame. Application Deadline: Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis starting on October 1, 2024 , and will continue until the positions are filled. Interested candidates should submit the following materials via the Interfolio system: Cover Letter: A detailed cover letter articulating how your qualifications and experience align with the Berthiaume Family Professorship, and how you will contribute to the University of Notre Dame’s mission. Curriculum Vitae: A complete and up-to-date CV. Research Statement: A two-page document outlining the impact and future direction of your research. Teaching and Mentoring Philosophy: A one-page statement describing your approach to teaching and mentoring students. References: Contact information for at least three references. For inquiries, please contact Dr. Patricia Champion , Associate Dean for Research, Graduate and Postdoctoral Studies, College of Science."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/172986": {
      "title": "Professor",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: Political Science Location: Notre Dame, IN The University of Notre Dame's Department of Political Science and the Keough School of Global Affairs are searching for one or more faculty members who specialize in the study of democracy. We expect the appointment to be at the level of Full Professor, but we will consider applications from advanced Associate Professors. We define the study of democracy broadly, as research on political regimes and regime change, challenges to democratic institutions, and political behavior. We have a preference for applicants with a research agenda in comparative politics, though we are open to researchers whose studies include the United States as a case.  Successful candidates will also be capable of excellent teaching and mentoring at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. Position duties include research, teaching, and service. A PhD is required in political science, any subfield of political science, other social science disciplines, or related fields. This search is conducted under the auspices of the Notre Dame Democracy Initiative, a significant investment from the Provost’s office that will provide multiple faculty lines and substantial research support. Those attracted to a university with a Catholic identity are particularly encouraged to apply. Information about Notre Dame, including our mission statement, is available at www.nd.edu. Applicants are asked to provide a CV and letter of interest. Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/182347": {
      "title": "Roth-Gibson Professor of Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering and Director, Hessert Aerospace Laboratories",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Engineering: Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering Location: University of Notre Dame The University of Notre Dame invites applications and nominations for a distinguished scholar and visionary leader to serve as the Roth-Gibson Professor of Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering and Director of the Hessert Aerospace Laboratories. This senior, faculty leadership role is intended for an internationally recognized aerospace researcher who will provide strategic direction and intellectual leadership to advance the laboratories as a world class hub for aerospace research, education, and collaboration. The Director will integrate and elevate the laboratory’s exceptional experimental, computational, and theoretical capabilities to address critical and emerging challenges in aerospace engineering. The successful candidate will cultivate a dynamic, interdisciplinary research environment that supports faculty, postdoctoral researchers, graduate and undergraduate students, and visiting scholars, while enhancing the global impact and reputation of Notre Dame’s aerospace enterprise. Key Responsibilities Provide overall leadership and long-term strategic direction for the Hessert Aerospace Laboratories. Develop a strategic plan for the development and utilization of the facilities and space that supports the faculty needs Build and sustain a premier research organization that integrates experimental, computational, and theoretical approaches. Enhance national and international visibility through high-impact research products, initiatives, partnerships, and scholarly leadership. Foster interdisciplinary collaboration among faculty, students, postdoctoral scholars, and external partners. Lead efforts to secure major external funding from federal agencies, industry, and foundations. Ensure the safe, effective, and innovative operation of facilities while guiding future infrastructure investments. Manage the staff resources to effectively support ongoing and developing research programs in an equitable and transparent manner . Champion educational and research training opportunities from undergraduate through postdoctoral levels. Mentor graduate students, postdoctoral researchers, junior faculty, and emerging leaders in aerospace engineering. Represent the Hessert Aerospace Laboratories within the University and to external stakeholders. Earned doctorate in aerospace engineering or a closely related field. Internationally recognized record of scholarly achievement and sustained research excellence. Demonstrated leadership experience in academic research environments, centers, or laboratories. Strong commitment to undergraduate and graduate education and mentorship. Eligibility for appointment at the senior (tenured) faculty rank. Experience leading large-scale, multidisciplinary research programs. Ability to secure major external research funding. Experience managing complex research facilities or shared infrastructure. Demonstrated commitment to fostering collaborative, inclusive, and high-impact research environments. This is an endowed, senior level faculty appointment, with compensation and start up package commensurate with qualifications and experience. The position carries substantial leadership responsibility, strategic influence, and institutional support. Additional information: Hessert Laboratory for Aerospace Research and Hessert at White Field Applicants are invited to provide any or all of the following: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Comprehensive Research Plan Statement of teaching philosophy and interests Contact information for three professional references Statement regarding potential engagement with and commitment to the mission of the University of Notre Dame Questions regarding this position may be directed to Professor Glen L. Niebur (gniebur@nd.edu)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187311": {
      "title": "Instructor/Assistant/Associate/Full Professor in Applied and Computational Mathematics and Statistics - Multiple Positions",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Science: Applied and Computational Mathematics and Statistics Location: Notre Dame The Department of Applied and Computational Mathematics and Statistics (ACMS) in the College of Science at the University of Notre Dame is accepting applications for multiple faculty positions, which are expected to be filled this year.  The search will focus on candidates appropriate for the rank of assistant professor, but exceptional candidates at the rank of associate professor or full professor will also be considered. These positions are supported by the Data, AI, and Computing Initiative, and the selected candidates will be affiliated with this Initiative and/or its research institutes or centers. Although we are searching broadly in all areas of applied and computational mathematics and statistics, preference will be given to applicants whose research is connected with at least one of the following areas: Data-driven approaches : physics-informed regularization, operator learning, state-space models, scientific machine learning, and algorithms to solve linear and nonlinear systems and differential equations. Additionally, research on optimization, uncertainty quantification, and machine learning techniques for multi-fidelity, multi-scale, and multi-physics problems is also of interest. Quantum computing : quantum algorithms, quantum machine learning, quantum advantage, and quantum data science. ACMS has research groups in applied mathematics, statistics and data science, and AI and machine learning, all featuring various multidisciplinary collaborations. ACMS offers a Bachelor of Science, a doctoral degree, a research master’s degree, and a professional master’s degree. Position duties include research, teaching, and service. The teaching load in ACMS is competitive, and the position is expected to begin in August 2027. Candidates at the entry level must have completed all requirements for a doctorate degree or have completed all but dissertation in applied mathematics, data science, mathematics, statistics, or a closely related field. Candidates at the advanced level must have a Ph.D. or relevant terminal degree in applied mathematics, data science, mathematics, statistics, or a closely related field. Ideal candidates will have a proven track record of successful multidisciplinary collaboration. We will begin reviewing completed applications on October 15, 2026, and continue accepting applications through November 20, 2026. Applications, including a cover letter, curriculum vitae, research statement, and teaching statement, should be submitted through the Interfolio system. Applicants should also arrange for at least three letters of recommendation to be submitted via Interfolio. Questions about the positions may be addressed to Department Chair, Jonathan Hauenstein, at hauenstein@nd.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187934": {
      "title": "Tenure-track Faculty in Theoretical Condensed Matter Physics",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Science: Physics and Astronomy Location: Notre Dame, IN The Department of Physics and Astronomy of the University of Notre Dame invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in theoretical condensed matter physics. The condensed matter group at Notre Dame consists of 10 experimental and 7 theoretical faculty members, specializing in hard condensed matter, quantum materials, complex systems, and biological physics. The applicant should complement current activities of the S tavropoulos Center for Complex Quantum Matter's mission is to synthesize materials of interest for novel technologies and to study them with cutting-edge experimental and theoretical methods. While the emphasis is on computational condensed matter theory, quantum information and tensor network, exceptional candidates from all subfields of theoretical condensed matter physics are encouraged to apply. We seek a faculty member committed to developing and sustaining an environment of excellence in research, teaching, and service. The successful candidate must demonstrate the ability to develop a highly successful research program, attract independent research funding, teach effectively at both the graduate and undergraduate levels, and engage with students from diverse backgrounds. Duties include teaching, research, and service. The position is expected to be filled at the assistant professor level, but applications from exceptional researchers at associate professor and professor levels will also be considered. The expected start date is August 2027. The Department of Physics and Astronomy at Notre Dame has 48 tenured and tenure-track faculty, 5 teaching faculty, and another 24 research and concurrent faculty, as well as professors of the practice; more than 120 graduate students; and ~100 undergraduate physics majors. Additional information about the Department and the College of Science can be found at http://physics.nd.edu and http://science.nd.edu respectively. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent advanced degree in Physics or a closely related field. Applicants should submit a curriculum vitae, list of publications, detailed research plans, and a statement of teaching and mentoring. Research statement: Should describe the candidate’s research program, including background information such as overall scientific goals and accomplishments to date and the specific research questions and plans to be pursued at Notre Dame. The statement will be read by both specialists in the candidate's research area and the departmental Committee on Appointments, which includes all tenured and tenure-track faculty members. The research statement should not exceed five pages. Teaching statement: Should outline the candidate’s past teaching experience. The teaching statement should not exceed three pages. Candidates must also arrange for at least three letters of recommendation. Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026 and will continue until the position has been filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188377": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor of Bioengineering",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Engineering: Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering Location: Notre Dame, Indiana Shape the Future of Bioengineering at Notre Dame The University of Notre Dame invites applications for an Associate or Full Professor in the broad field of Bioengineering. We seek an innovative and accomplished researcher whose work bridges biology and engineering and who is excited to build a high-impact research program addressing some of the most important challenges in human health, biotechnology, and society. . The search is intentionally interdisciplinary, bringing together faculty from the Departments of Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering, Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering, Electrical Engineering, and Civil and Environmental Engineering and Earth Sciences. This collaborative structure provides an opportunity to develop research partnerships across disciplines. We are particularly interested in researchers whose work is advancing the frontiers of bioengineering in areas including: Tissue Engineering, Disease Models, and Stem Cell Research Developing innovative approaches to understand, model, repair, and regenerate biological systems and address complex diseases. Diagnostics, Devices, and Distributed Health Creating innovative diagnostic technologies and biomedical devices, particularly those that expand access to healthcare and address the needs of rural, remote, and distributed healthcare settings. Emerging Frontiers in Bioengineering Exploring new applications including areas such as space bioengineering, biocomputing, and other emerging technologies. We welcome candidates whose research extends beyond these areas and who bring new perspectives, technologies, and opportunities for collaboration to Notre Dame. An Environment Built for Collaboration At Notre Dame, researchers have the opportunity to collaborate across engineering, science, medicine, data science, and global health through a network of institutes and centers. Faculty may engage with interdisciplinary communities including: Bioengineering and Life Sciences Initiative: (BELS Initiative ) Berthiaume Institute for Precision Health ( BIPH ) Harper Cancer Research Institute ( HRCI ) Boler-Parseghian Center for Rare and Neglected Diseases ( CRND ) Eck Institute for Global Health ( EIGH ) Lucy Family Institute for Data and Society ( Lucy Institute ) Keck Center for Transgene Research ( Keck Center ) Warren Center for Drug Discovery ( Warren Center ) Center for Stem Cells and Regenerative Medicine ( SCReM ) The successful candidate will be expected to establish and lead an internationally recognized research program, attract significant external funding, teach and mentor undergraduate and graduate students, and contribute to the intellectual and professional life of the University. We are looking for a scholar who will not simply participate in Notre Dame's bioengineering community, but help shape its future. Candidates should demonstrate: A strong and sustained record of scholarly achievement in bioengineering or a related discipline. A research portfolio demonstrating meaningful contributions at the intersection of engineering and biology. The potential for national and international distinction. A record of, or strong potential for, developing an externally funded research program. Excellence or demonstrated potential for excellence in undergraduate and graduate education. A commitment to interdisciplinary research and collaboration. A willingness to contribute to the broader research and academic community. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in engineering or a related discipline and have an established, independent research program at the intersection of engineering and biology. Successful candidates will have a sustained record of research excellence, scholarly achievement, and external funding, as well as demonstrated leadership and contributions to their field. This search is limited to candidates at the Associate Professor or Full Professor level. We are seeking established scholars who have demonstrated the ability to lead an independent research program, secure external funding, mentor students and researchers, and contribute to the field of bioengineering at Notre Dame. For best consideration, please submit your application prior to November 1, 2026. All other applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until December 1, 2026. Applicants are invited to submit some or all of the following materials: Cover letter Curriculum vitae Comprehensive research plan Statement of teaching philosophy and interests Apply in confidence, references contacted only with your permission Statement regarding potential engagement with and commitment to the mission of the University of Notre Dame"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188579": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Film History",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: Film, Television and Theatre Location: Notre Dame, IN The Department of Film, Television, and Theatre (FTT) at the University of Notre Dame is seeking a candidate for an appointment as a tenure-track assistant professor of film history with a starting date of August 2027. While any area of film history is welcome, we would particularly welcome scholars in areas such as animation, industry, African American film, and national cinemas from the Global South and scholars whose work aligns with one or more of the university’s interdisciplinary units such as the Liu Institute for Asia and Asian Studies, the Institute for Latino Studies, Africana Studies, and/or Gender Studies. Responsibilities include undergraduate teaching (2 courses per semester) in the candidate’s area of expertise as well as core courses in the film concentration and/or service courses in the College of Arts and Letters. Candidates at the entry level must have completed all requirements for a PhD degree or have completed all but dissertation in film studies or a closely related field. Candidates at the advanced assistant professor level must have a PhD in film studies or a closely related field. Candidates must have experience in or describe their potential for successful college-level teaching in a liberal arts environment. Candidates must articulate a clear research trajectory and experience in or describe their potential for publishing in top-tier academic journals and presses. Job applicants should submit: ● a curriculum vitae listing three references with contact information ● a cover letter describing the candidate’s: research, with a description of methods and goals; future career trajectory and plans for future projects; teaching experience and pedagogic principles; and how their courses and pedagogy will enhance FTT’s curriculum. The committee will continue to vet applications until the position is filled, but candidates should send materials no later than 1 November, 2026 for fullest consideration. Questions about the position can be directed to Professor Jim Collins, Chair of the Search Committee, email: collins@nd.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188580": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of the Practice in Musical Theatre Writing",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: Film, Television and Theatre Location: Notre Dame, IN The Department of Film, Television, and Theatre (FTT) at the University of Notre Dame is seeking a candidate for an appointment as an assistant professor of the practice in musical theatre writing, with a starting date of August 2027.  The Department is especially interested in candidates with primary expertise in songwriting and lyric composition, with knowledge of various musical styles, including pop idioms, and a commitment to engaging with diverse songwriting traditions. Must be proficient in and able to train students in music-writing software such as Sibelius, MuseScore, and/or Logic Pro. Responsibilities include undergraduate teaching (3 courses per semester) in the department’s Musical Theatre Minor program, including such courses as songwriting, lyric writing, and musical theatre song analysis; and elective courses in orchestration and arranging, music direction, or other areas of specialization.  Successful candidates may also teach in the New Works Lab, a course in which students write a new musical; advise student capstone projects; and/or assist on department Musical Theatre productions (both new and existing musicals). This position also offers opportunities for collaboration with faculty across departments and with university-wide institutes and the Arts Initiative. Candidates must have an MFA, MA, or MM in Musical Theatre, Musical Theatre Writing, Music, or related fields; or a PhD or DMA in Music Theory/Composition. Candidates must have a demonstrable track record of successful professional musical theatre productions. Candidates must have experience in or demonstrable potential for successful college-level teaching in a liberal arts environment. Job applicants should submit: ● a curriculum vitae listing three references with contact information ● a cover letter describing the candidate’s: Approach to musical theatre writing; Representative successful past productions; future career trajectory and plans for future projects; teaching experience and pedagogic principles; and how their courses and pedagogy will enhance FTT’s curriculum. The committee will continue to vet applications until the position is filled, but candidates should send materials no later than 1 November, 2026 for fullest consideration. Questions about the position can be directed to Associate Professor Tarryn Chun, Chair of the Search Committee, email: tchun@nd.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188748": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: Economics Location: Notre Dame, Indiana, US The Department of Economics at the University of Notre Dame seeks applicants for up to three tenure-track assistant professor positions. Duties will include teaching, research, and service. Candidates with research interests in Macroeconomics, Economic Theory, and/or Econometrics will receive priority consideration. Entry-level candidates must have completed all Ph.D. requirements or be on track to complete a Ph.D. in Economics or a closely related field by July 1, 2027. Candidates at the advanced assistant level must have a Ph.D. in Economics or a closely related field. Applications submitted by November 15, 2026, will receive priority consideration. Questions may be directed to Christopher Cronin ( ccronin1@nd.edu )."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188750": {
      "title": "Professor",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: Economics Location: Notre Dame, Indiana, US The Department of Economics at the University of Notre Dame invites applications for a tenured Full Professor position beginning in Fall 2027, with the possibility of an endowed, named chair, for a scholar whose research program will advance the mission of the university’s Poverty Initiative. Candidates should have a record of research excellence with a strong emphasis on issues related to poverty, a proven capacity for leadership, evidence of superlative teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels, and a capacity to support and shape exciting new university programs. We especially seek candidates whose work has strong synergies with existing faculty research and initiatives across campus. Duties include research, teaching, and service. A PhD in Economics or a closely related field is required. Review of applications will commence immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Questions may be directed to Christopher Cronin ( ccronin1@nd.edu )."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188751": {
      "title": "Professor, Open Rank",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: Economics Location: Notre Dame, Indiana, US The Department of Economics at the University of Notre Dame invites applications for an open-rank, tenure-track position to begin in the Fall of 2027 whose research program will support the Strengthening Families Research Initiative. We especially welcome applications from c andidates at the ranks of Associate or Full Professor. Candidates should have a record of research excellence with a strong emphasis on issues related to the economics of families, fertility, child well-being, and/or social policy. The ideal candidate will also have a track record of superlative teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels, and a capacity to support and shape a university program. Duties include research, teaching, and service. A PhD in Economics or a closely related field is required. Review of applications will commence immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Questions may be directed to Christopher Cronin ( ccronin1@nd.edu )."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188951": {
      "title": "Tenure-track Assistant Professor in Philosophy, Program of Liberal Studies",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: Program of Liberal Studies Location: Notre Dame, IN The Program of Liberal Studies (PLS) seeks to hire a tenure-track assistant professor in Philosophy beginning August 2027. Duties will include research, teaching and service. The area of research specialization is the text-based approach to History of Philosophy, with a preference for the Pre-Modern or Early Modern periods. We are especially interested in applications from candidates with a broad liberal arts background. Teaching responsibility will be a 2-2 course load, including the Program's Great Books seminars and its three philosophy courses (tutorials): Philosophical Inquiry, Ethics, and Metaphysics and Epistemology (see our website, http://pls.nd.edu , for course descriptions). The candidate may also contribute to other disciplinary-based courses in the department as appropriate and will advise undergraduate senior theses. PLS is a multidisciplinary, liberal arts department with roughly 125 undergraduate majors. Founded in 1950, it has its own tenure-line faculty drawn from different disciplines and committed to an integrated program of studies grounded in the reading of core primary texts. PLS students move through a seminar sequence from the ancients to the contemporary world and also take subject-specific classes in literature, philosophy, theology, fine arts, intellectual and cultural history, political theory, and the natural and social sciences. All PLS faculty teach seminars, as well as courses in their respective disciplines. Candidates at the entry level must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D. degree or have completed all but dissertation in Philosophy or a closely related field with an expectation to have completed all requirements for the Ph.D. degree by August 2027. Candidates at the advanced level must have a Ph.D. in Philosophy or a closely related field. Strong publication program, evidence of superior teaching and readiness to lead seminars across periods and fields, and a commitment to undergraduate liberal arts education are expected. Salary and research support are more than competitive. The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. Please submit a cover letter, CV, three confidential letters of recommendation, and a scholarly writing sample (maximum of circa 10,000 words). Before applying, candidates are strongly encouraged to consult the department website ( http://pls.nd.edu ) and the university mission statement ( https://www.nd.edu/about/mission/ ). Questions about the position may be addressed to Prof. Christopher Chowrimootoo, Chairperson, Program of Liberal Studies, prlibst@nd.edu. Deadline: Apply by October 1, 2026, to guarantee full consideration, though applications will be considered until the position is filled. This institution is using Interfolio's ByCommittee to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge: https://apply.interfolio.com/188951"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190409": {
      "title": "Tenure-track Faculty Position in Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Engineering: Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Location: Notre Dame, Indiana The Department of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering ( http://cbe.nd.edu ) at the University of Notre Dame invites applications for tenure-track assistant professor positions from individuals with research interests at the broad intersection of biology and chemical engineering. Exceptional established candidates may also be considered for appointment at the Associate Professor or Professor rank. The successful candidate will develop and lead an innovative research program that addresses forefront challenges in biomolecular, biochemical, biotechnology, or bioprocess engineering, including but not limited to synthetic biology, biosensing, systems biology, metabolic engineering, and microbial engineering . Position duties include research, teaching, and service. The position is expected to begin in July 2027 . Applications will be reviewed beginning on October 21, 2026 . The environment for biomolecular engineering at Notre Dame is enriched by numerous cross-disciplinary institutes and centers, including: Bioengineering and Life Sciences Initiative ( BELS Initiative ) Berthiaume Institute for Precision Health ( BIPH ) Harper Cancer Research Institute ( HRCI ) Boler-Parseghian Center for Rare and Neglected Diseases ( CRND ) Eck Institute for Global Health ( EIGH ) Lucy Family Institute for Data and Society ( Lucy Institute ) Keck Center for Transgene Research ( Keck Center ) Warren Center for Drug Discovery ( Warren Center ) Center for Stem Cells and Regenerative Medicine ( SCReM ) Candidates at the entry level must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D. degree or have completed all but dissertation in Chemical Engineering or a closely related field with degree expected before July 1, 2027. Candidates at the advanced level must have a Ph.D. in Chemical Engineering or a closely related field. Candidates should provide the following: cover letter; curriculum vitae; comprehensive research plan; statement of teaching philosophy and interests; and contact information for three professional references. Candidates are also invited to include a statement regarding potential engagement with and commitment to the mission of the University of Notre Dame. All materials should be submitted through the application website. Applications will be considered on a rolling basis upon receipt, with priority review of applications received by October 21, 2026 , and a final deadline of December 1, 2026 ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190459": {
      "title": "Senior Endowed Chair in US History",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: History Location: Notre Dame, Indiana The History Department of Notre Dame invites applications for an endowed senior chair (full professor) in United States history. We welcome applications from full professors working in any period or specialization of United States history.  Position duties include research, teaching, and service. The successful candidate should have an international scholarly reputation as well as a record of excellence in teaching and service. The candidate is expected to take a leadership role in furthering the research, teaching, and service missions of the Department of History and the University of Notre Dame. Candidates must have a PhD in US History or a closely related field. To apply, candidates should submit a letter of application and curriculum vitae through Interfolio. Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026 , and will continue until the position is filled. Please address any inquiries to Professor Jon Coleman ( Jon.T.Coleman.63@nd.edu ), Search Committee Chair. Information about Notre Dame is available here , and about the department at history.nd.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190464": {
      "title": "Open Rank; Tenured and Tenure-Track Position in Accounting",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: Mendoza College of Business: Accountancy Location: Notre Dame, IN THE UNIVERSITY OF NOTRE DAME, Department of Accountancy , invites applications for a position o pening at all levels, Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor. The appointments are expected to begin July 1, 2027 . Al l candidates should have a strong commitment to high-quality scholarly research and excellence in teaching at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. Salary is very competitive and research support is excellent. Position duties include teaching, research, and service. Candidates at the entry-level must have completed all requirements for a PhD degree or have completed all but dissertation in Accounting with degree expected by July 1, 2027. Candidates at the advanced level must have a PhD in Accounting. Please submit a letter of interest, with clear indication of position being applied for; a curriculum vita; three letters of recommendation; report of courses taught and evidence of teaching effectiveness; and any other supporting documents, such as curriculum development materials."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190478": {
      "title": "Non-Tenure Track Teaching Professor in Accountancy",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: Mendoza College of Business: Accountancy Location: Notre Dame, IN THE UNIVERSITY OF NOTRE DAME , Department of Accountancy , invites applicants for a non-tenure track faculty teaching appointment beginning fall 2027. The successful candidate will be capable of teaching taxation in undergraduate and graduate programs. Preference will be given to candidates with tax experience.  The deadline for applications is February 1, 2027. An ideal candidate would (i) hold a PhD in Accounting or have significant and relevant professional experience; (ii) possess a strong commitment to teaching and academic engagement; (iii) have professional certification (e.g., CPA, CFA, CIA); and (iv) be skilled in effectively working with others. Please submit a cover letter, curriculum vita, three letters of recommendation, mailing address, and evidence of teaching ability. Applications will be considered complete once all the required documents have been uploaded.  Applications must be completed by February 1, 2027 for full consideration."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190844": {
      "title": "Open-Rank, Open Field, Tenure Track or Tenured, Sociology",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: Sociology Location: Notre Dame, Indiana The Department of Sociology at the University of Notre Dame invites applications for an open-rank (tenured or tenure-track) position set to begin in the fall of 2027. Position titles may include Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor.  The area of specialization is open.  Duties include research, teaching, and service. Our department has a reputation for being both collegial and supportive without sacrificing ambition. The Department of Sociology at Notre Dame has long-standing excellence in the sociology of culture, education, movements and politics, religion, and theory. In recent years, we have established strengths in several sociological areas, including family, gender & sexuality, global & transnational, health, organizations & institutions, race & ethnicity, and stratification. We especially welcome applications from scholars who contribute to one or more of the department's existing strengths while also helping us expand into new areas of expertise. The faculty embraces a range of innovative methodological approaches and has expertise that covers the globe. Applications will be evaluated on a rolling basis with full consideration given to applications received by September 1, 2026 or until the position is filled. Further information about the Department of Sociology may be found at https://sociology.nd.edu . If you have questions, please contact Professor Rory McVeigh ( rmcveigh@nd.edu ). Candidates at the entry level must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D. degree or have completed all but dissertation in Sociology or a closely related field. Candidates at the advanced assistant, associate, or full professor level must have a Ph.D. in Sociology or a closely related field. The University of Notre Dame and its Department of Sociology are committed to teaching excellence and mentorship at both the graduate and undergraduate levels. The following application materials should be submitted through the Interfolio system: a letter of application, CV, a research statement, a teaching statement, course evaluations (if available), and writing samples of representative work–if possible, please include at least one solo-authored writing sample. For untenured candidates, please submit at least three letters of reference. For already tenured faculty, we will ask long-listed candidates for a minimum of three letters of reference at that time. Questions about the position may be addressed to Professor Rory McVeigh ( rmcveigh@nd.edu ). If you have any technical questions about submitting the application, please reach out to the department administrator, Rebecca Overmyer (rovermye@nd.edu). Salary and Benefits Appointment salary and rank are competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. The University offers an excellent benefits package. Environment The University of Notre Dame is a highly selective national Catholic teaching and research university in northern Indiana about ninety miles from Chicago. Approximately 9,000 undergraduates and 4,200 graduate students pursue a broad range of studies. The University of Notre Dame is a Catholic academic community of higher learning, animated from its origins by the Congregation of Holy Cross. The University is dedicated to the pursuit and sharing of truth for its own sake. As a Catholic university, one of its distinctive goals is to provide a forum where, through free inquiry and open discussion, the various lines of Catholic thought may intersect with all the forms of knowledge found in the arts, sciences, professions, and every other area of human scholarship and creativity. For more information about the University of Notre Dame, please visit the University of Notre Dame homepage at http://nd.edu/ ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190846": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Quantitative Sociology (tenure-track)",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: Sociology Location: Notre Dame, Indiana The Department of Sociology at the University of Notre Dame invites applications for an assistant professor (tenure-track) position with expertise in quantitative sociology set to begin in the fall of 2027. We especially welcome applications from scholars with proven expertise in AI-assisted, computational, network, or advanced statistical methods. We seek candidates who are advancing the methodological frontier in these areas by developing, extending, or creatively innovating quantitative approaches to sociological research. Duties include teaching, research, and service. This position is affiliated with the Data, AI, and Computing (DAC) Initiative and will have teaching responsibilities with both the department of Sociology and the College of Arts & Letters Data Science programs. The Department of Sociology at Notre Dame has long-standing excellence in the sociology of culture, education, movements and politics, religion, and theory. In recent years, we have established strengths in several other sociological areas, including family, gender & sexuality, global & transnational, health, organizations & institutions, race & ethnicity, and stratification. The faculty embraces a range of innovative methodological approaches and has expertise that covers the globe. We are collegial, supportive, and ambitious. Applications will be evaluated on a rolling basis with full consideration given to applications received by September 1, 2026 or until the position is filled. Further information about the Department of Sociology may be found at https://sociology.nd.edu . If you have questions, please contact Professor Sarah Mustillo ( smustill@nd.edu ). Candidates at the entry level must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D. degree or have completed all but dissertation in Sociology or a closely related field. Candidates at the advanced assistant professor level must have a Ph.D. in Sociology or a closely related field. The University of Notre Dame and its Department of Sociology are committed to teaching excellence and mentorship at both the graduate and undergraduate levels. The following application materials should be submitted through the Interfolio system: a letter of application, CV, three letters of reference, a research statement, a teaching statement, course evaluations (if available), and a recent sample of representative publication–if possible, please include at least one solo-authored writing sample. Questions about the positions may be addressed to Professor Sarah Mustillo ( smustill@nd.edu ). Salary and Benefits Appointment salary and rank are competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. The University offers an excellent benefits package. Environment The University of Notre Dame is a highly selective national Catholic teaching and research university in northern Indiana about ninety miles from Chicago. Approximately 9,000 undergraduates and 4,200 graduate students pursue a broad range of studies. The University of Notre Dame is a Catholic academic community of higher learning, animated from its origins by the Congregation of Holy Cross. The University is dedicated to the pursuit and sharing of truth for its own sake. As a Catholic university, one of its distinctive goals is to provide a forum where, through free inquiry and open discussion, the various lines of Catholic thought may intersect with all the forms of knowledge found in the arts, sciences, professions, and every other area of human scholarship and creativity. For more information about the University of Notre Dame, please visit the University of Notre Dame homepage at http://nd.edu/ ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191572": {
      "title": "Assistant Research Professor",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: Economics Location: Notre Dame, Indiana, US The Wilson Sheehan Lab for Economic Opportunities (LEO) within the University of Notre Dame’s Department of Economics seeks research faculty at the rank of assistant research professor. There are no teaching responsibilities. The position is non-tenure track. Assistant research professors are ordinarily appointed for one-year, renewable terms; associate and full research professors are ordinarily multi-year terms. LEO is a research center dedicated to reducing poverty and improving lives in the United States through evidence-based programs and policies. Learn more at leo.nd.edu . A PhD in Economics or a closely related field is required. ABD eligible if PhD awarded by July 1, 2027. The ideal candidate has evidence of excellence in research, with experience in the design, execution, or evaluation of randomized controlled trials. The successful candidate will develop and maintain a portfolio of LEO-related research projects. Applicants should submit materials to Interfolio. Applications submitted by November 15, 2026 will receive full consideration. Questions may be directed to Eryn Ray ( eray@nd.edu )."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191574": {
      "title": "Associate/Full Research Professor",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: Economics Location: Notre Dame, Indiana, US The Wilson Sheehan Lab for Economic Opportunities (LEO) within the University of Notre Dame’s Department of Economics seeks research faculty at the rank of associate or full research professor. There are no teaching responsibilities. The position is non-tenure track. Associate and full research professors are ordinarily appointed for multi-year, renewable terms. LEO is a research center dedicated to reducing poverty and improving lives in the United States through evidence-based programs and policies. Learn more at leo.nd.edu . A PhD in Economics or a closely related field is required. The ideal candidate has 5+ years experience, a well-developed and ongoing research program, and proven capacity in the design, execution, or evaluation of randomized controlled trials. The successful candidate will develop and maintain a portfolio of LEO-related research projects. Applicants should submit materials to Interfolio. Applications submitted by October 1, 2026 will receive full consideration. Questions may be directed to Eryn Ray (eray@nd.edu)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191711": {
      "title": "Philosophy | Open Area | Endowed Professorship (tenured)",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: Philosophy Location: Notre Dame, IN UNIVERSITY OF NOTRE DAME, Notre Dame, IN. The Department of Philosophy seeks to appoint a chairholder to the McMahon-Hank Professor of Philosophy. We welcome qualified applicants working in any subfield of Philosophy. Appointment to begin Fall Semester 2027. Rank: Full professor, endowed chair (tenured). AOS: Open. AOC: Open. Standard teaching load in the department is 2:1 (3 classes per academic year). Duties include teaching, research, and service. Salary competitive. Semester system. Candidates must have a Ph.D. in Philosophy or a closely related field. Please submit a CV and brief cover letter. Rolling application deadline, with review starting October 15, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191754": {
      "title": "Electrical Engineering, Tenured or Tenure-track faculty Position",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Engineering: Electrical Engineering Location: Main Campus The Department of Electrical Engineering at the University of Notre Dame invites applications for tenure-track and tenured faculty positions at all levels and in all areas of Electrical Engineering. Position titles may include Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Full Professor.  The department is particularly interested in candidates whose research complements and extends existing strengths in wireless communications and networks, autonomous decision and control, signal and/or image processing, power systems and the electric grid, smart city applications, and cryogenic CMOS and low-temperature electronics, including circuits and devices for quantum computing and sensing. Duties will include research, teaching, and service. The Department offers Ph.D., MS, and undergraduate degrees. Tenured and tenure-track faculty members are expected to excel in classroom teaching, lead highly visible research projects that attract substantial external funding, advise graduate students, and serve the profession and the University. More information about the department can be found at https://ee.nd.edu/. Applicants are invited to provide any or all of the following: cover letter; curriculum vitae; comprehensive research plan; statement of teaching philosophy and interests; contact information for three professional references; and statement regarding potential engagement with and commitment to the mission of the University of Notre Dame. To guarantee full consideration, applications must be received by November 30, 2026; however, applications will be accepted until December 15, 2026. The University of Notre Dame is situated within the South Bend, Indiana metropolitan area, which has a population of approximately 325,000. South Bend is recognized for its relative affordability and strategic geographic location, approximately 75 minutes east of Chicago and 45 minutes from the Lake Michigan shoreline and Indiana Dunes National Park. These locations are accessible via the South Shore Line commuter rail. The city is also served by the South Bend International Airport, offering convenient air travel options. Candidates at the entry level must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D. degree or have completed all but dissertation in Electrical Engineering or a closely related field by August 1, 2027. Candidates at the advanced assistant, associate, or full professor level must have a Ph.D. in Electrical Engineering or a closely related field. Applicants are invited to provide any or all of the following: cover letter; curriculum vitae; comprehensive research plan; statement of teaching philosophy and interests; contact information for three professional references; and statement regarding potential engagement with and commitment to the mission of the University of Notre Dame. All materials should be submitted to the application website."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191855": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Position in the Finance Department",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: Mendoza College of Business: Finance Location: Notre Dame, IN The University of Notre Dame, Department of Finance, is looking for applicants in all areas of finance to fill one or more tenure-track positions in the Finance Department. Positions can be at the untenured assistant, tenured associate, or tenured full professor level depending on qualifications. Position duties include publishing in leading finance journals, teaching well at the undergraduate and graduate levels, and contributing to the intellectual life of the college. We offer competitive salary and benefits and generous research support. The University of Notre Dame , located in South Bend, Indiana, is consistently ranked among the top 25 undergraduate institutions in the U.S. The university has approximately 13,000 students, with undergraduates accounting for 70% of the student body. As part of the University’s Mendoza College of Business, the finance department contributes to a top-ranked undergraduate business program and several highly regarded graduate programs, including MBA, executive MBA, and Masters of Science in Finance programs. Finance faculty and students also benefit from close collaboration with the Notre Dame Institute for Global Investing (NDIGI) and the Fitzgerald Institute for Real Estate (FIRE). For more information about the Mendoza College of Business or the finance department, visit https://mendoza.nd.edu/ or https://mendoza.nd.edu/research-faculty/academic-departments/finance/ . Candidates at the entry level must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D. degree in Finance or a closely related field or have completed all but the dissertation with an expected dissertation defense date prior to August 1, 2027. Candidates at the advanced assistant, associate, or full professor level must have a Ph.D. in Finance or a closely related field. Applications for the Tenure Track Position must be submitted via Interfolio. Applications should include: 1) a cover letter indicating the candidate’s interest in a position at the University of Notre Dame; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) one job market paper; 4) three confidential letters of recommendation. Additional working papers or publications may also be submitted. All documents must be in pdf format. We will conduct online interviews prior to the January AFA meetings. For full consideration, applications should be submitted no later than November 20, 2026. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192059": {
      "title": "University Chair Position in the Finance Department",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: Mendoza College of Business: Finance Location: Notre Dame, IN The University of Notre Dame, Department of Finance, is looking for applicants to fill a University Chair position in the Finance Department. This position is open to highly accomplished senior scholars with research and teaching interests in any area of finance. Rank is at the tenured full professor level with a further distinction of a university chair professorship. Position duties include publishing in leading finance journals, teaching well at the undergraduate and graduate levels, and contributing to the intellectual life of the college. We offer competitive salary and benefits and generous research support. The University of Notre Dame , located in South Bend, Indiana, is consistently ranked among the top 25 undergraduate institutions in the U.S. The university has approximately 13,000 students, with undergraduates accounting for 70% of the student body. As part of the University’s Mendoza College of Business, the finance department contributes to a top-ranked undergraduate business program and several highly regarded graduate programs, including MBA, executive MBA, and Masters of Science in Finance programs. Finance faculty and students also benefit from close collaboration with the Notre Dame Institute for Global Investing (NDIGI) and the Fitzgerald Institute for Real Estate (FIRE). For more information about the Mendoza College of Business or the finance department, visit https://mendoza.nd.edu/ or https://mendoza.nd.edu/research-faculty/academic-departments/finance/ . The successful candidate will be a renowned scholar with a demonstrated record of excellence in research, teaching, and service.  Candidates must also have a Ph.D. in Finance or closely related field. Applications for the University Chair Position must be submitted via Interfolio. Applications should include: 1) a cover letter indicating the candidate’s interest in a position at the University of Notre Dame; 2) a curriculum vitae. Additional documentation of teaching performance or letters of recommendation may also be requested. Our intention is to begin holding campus interviews by late January 2027. For full consideration, applications should be submitted no later than December 31, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192077": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Real Estate Position in the Finance Department",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: Mendoza College of Business: Finance Location: Notre Dame, IN The University of Notre Dame, Department of Finance, looking for applicants who have attained or are completing a Ph.D. in finance or economics and whose research is related to housing and real estate finance to fill a tenure-track position in the Finance Department. The position can be at the untenured assistant, tenured associate, or tenured full professor level depending on qualifications. Position duties include publishing in leading finance journals, teaching well at the undergraduate and graduate levels, and contributing to the intellectual life of the college. We offer competitive salary and benefits and generous research support. The University of Notre Dame , located in South Bend, Indiana, is consistently ranked among the top 25 undergraduate institutions in the U.S. The university has approximately 13,000 students, with undergraduates accounting for 70% of the student body. As part of the University’s Mendoza College of Business, the finance department contributes to a top-ranked undergraduate business program and several highly regarded graduate programs, including MBA, executive MBA, and Masters of Science in Finance programs. Finance faculty and students also benefit from close collaboration with the Notre Dame Institute for Global Investing (NDIGI) and the Fitzgerald Institute for Real Estate (FIRE). For more information about the Mendoza College of Business or the finance department, visit https://mendoza.nd.edu/ or https://mendoza.nd.edu/research-faculty/academic-departments/finance/ . Candidates at the entry level must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D. degree in Finance or a closely related field or have completed all but the dissertation with an expected dissertation defense date prior to August 1, 2027. Candidates at the advanced assistant, associate, or full professor level must have a Ph.D. in Finance or a closely related field. Applications for the Tenure Track Position must be submitted via Interfolio. Applications should include: 1) a cover letter indicating the candidate’s interest in a position at the University of Notre Dame; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) one job market paper; 4) three confidential letters of recommendation. Additional working papers or publications may also be submitted. All documents must be in pdf format. We will conduct online interviews prior to the January AFA meetings. For full consideration, applications should be submitted no later than November 20, 2026. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192167": {
      "title": "Real Estate Teaching Professor Position in the Finance Department",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: Mendoza College of Business: Finance Location: Notre Dame, IN The University of Notre Dame, Department of Finance, is looking for applicants in the fields of housing and real estate finance to fill a teaching faculty position (non-tenure-track) in the Finance Department. The position can be at the assistant, associate, or full teaching professor level depending on qualifications. Position duties include outstanding teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels, and contributing to the intellectual life of the college. We offer competitive salary and benefits, and generous resources for professional development. The University of Notre Dame , located in South Bend, Indiana, is consistently ranked among the top 25 undergraduate institutions in the U.S. The University has approximately 13,000 students, with undergraduates accounting for 70% of the student body. As part of the University’s Mendoza College of Business, the finance department contributes to a top-ranked undergraduate business program and several highly regarded graduate programs, including MBA, executive MBA, and Masters of Science in Finance programs. Finance faculty and students also benefit from close collaboration with the Notre Dame Institute for Global Investing (NDIGI) and the Fitzgerald Institute for Real Estate (FIRE). For more information about the Mendoza College of Business or the finance department, visit https://mendoza.nd.edu/ or https://mendoza.nd.edu/research-faculty/academic-departments/finance/ . Candidates should have a demonstrated record of excellence in teaching in related subject matter. The ideal candidate will have significant relevant industry experience and/or hold a Ph.D. in finance or a related field. Applications for the Teaching Professor Position must be submitted via Interfolio. Applications should include: 1) a cover letter indicating the candidate’s interest in a position at the University of Notre Dame; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) teaching evaluations (if available). Additional documentation, including letters of recommendation, may also be requested. Our intention is to begin holding campus interviews in November 2026. For full consideration, applications should be submitted no later than November 1, 2026. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192169": {
      "title": "Teaching Professor Position in the Finance Department",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: Mendoza College of Business: Finance Location: Notre Dame, IN The University of Notre Dame, Department of Finance, is looking for applicants in all areas of finance to fill one or more teaching faculty positions (non-tenure-track) in the Finance Department. Positions can be at the assistant, associate, or full teaching professor level depending on qualifications. Position duties include outstanding teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels, and contributing to the intellectual life of the college. We offer competitive salary and benefits, and generous resources for professional development. The University of Notre Dame , located in South Bend, Indiana, is consistently ranked among the top 25 undergraduate institutions in the U.S. The University has approximately 13,000 students, with undergraduates accounting for 70% of the student body. As part of the University’s Mendoza College of Business, the finance department contributes to a top-ranked undergraduate business program and several highly regarded graduate programs, including MBA, executive MBA, and Masters of Science in Finance programs. Finance faculty and students also benefit from close collaboration with the Notre Dame Institute for Global Investing (NDIGI) and the Fitzgerald Institute for Real Estate (FIRE). For more information about the Mendoza College of Business or the finance department, visit https://mendoza.nd.edu/ or https://mendoza.nd.edu/research-faculty/academic-departments/finance/ . Candidates should have a demonstrated record of excellence in teaching across a range of topics and program levels. The ideal candidate will have significant relevant industry experience and/or hold a Ph.D. in finance or a related field. Applications for the Teaching Professor Position must be submitted via Interfolio. Applications should include: 1) a cover letter indicating the candidate’s interest in a position at the University of Notre Dame; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) teaching evaluations (if available). Additional documentation, including letters of recommendation, may also be requested. Our intention is to begin holding campus interviews in November 2026. For full consideration, applications should be submitted no later than November 1, 2026. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192535": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Science: Biological Sciences Location: University of Notre Dame Position Description The Department of Biological Sciences at the University of Notre Dame invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track faculty position in Microbiology . The position is expected to be filled at the assistant professor level, but applications from exceptional researchers at associate professor and professor levels will also be considered. We seek a forward-thinking, collaborative microbiologist driven to address fundamental and  impactful questions in microbial biology. We are particularly interested in candidates pushing the frontiers of microbial pathogenesis, host-microbe interactions, or antimicrobial resistance. Required: Ph.D., M.D. or equivalent doctoral degree in Microbiology, Biological Sciences, Molecular Biology, Immunology, or a closely related field. Postdoctoral training demonstrating research excellence and productivity through a track record of high-impact peer-reviewed publications. A clear vision for establishing an independent, extramurally funded research program. Strong commitment to undergraduate and graduate teaching and mentorship. About the University, Department & Campus Resources Notre Dame is an R1 research institution and a member of the AAU, combining world class scientific infrastructure with a deeply mission-driven academic environment. The Department of Biological Sciences at Notre Dame is a vibrant community of over 300 individuals including 159 graduate students and 63 faculty and serves as a central hub for life science education and research, focusing on the fundamental mechanisms of living systems to address grand challenges in human and environmental health. The department brings together world-class faculty, post-doctoral fellows, graduate students, and undergraduates to collaborate across multidisciplinary areas such as microbiology, immunology, cancer biology, ecology, evolution, genomics, and neurobiology. Through state-of-the-art facilities and strong affiliations with key research institutes, the department emphasizes integrative inquiry aimed at finding real-world solutions for disease prevention, climate change, and biodiversity conservation. Excellent Institutional Ecosystem: Faculty benefit from collaboration with Notre Dame's Centers and Institutes and Initiatives including the Eck Institute for Global Health, the Warren Center for Drug Discovery, the Harper Cancer Center, the Environmental Change Initiative, the Rare Disease Institute, the Bioengineering and Life Sciences and Just Transformations for Sustainability Initiatives. Opportunities for collaboration also include the NIH-funded Indiana Clinical and Translational Sciences Institute. Access to state-of-the-art campus core facilities: On campus access to the Genomics and Bioinformatics Core Facility (GBCF), Imaging and Flow Cytometry Core, Mass Spectrometry and Proteomics Facility, and Freimann Life Science Center, including BSL3/ABSL3 resources). Comprehensive Faculty Mentoring and Support: We are committed to supporting your success. New faculty received mentoring committees, tailored teaching loads to support establishment of research programs, and competitive start up packages and support to help recruit graduate students and postdoctoral trainees. Exceptional Quality of Life: Notre Dame is consistently ranked as one of the top employers in the United States and provides a top-tier Total Rewards package , including an exceptional retirement match, tuition benefits for dependent children, dual-career partner assistance, an on-campus Wellness Center & Pharmacy, and fully funded access to specialized care networks (Maven for family planning/menopause, Summus for global specialist second opinions, Vivvi for backup childcare/tutoring, and Cariloop for dedicated caregiving coaching). All applications must be submitted electronically through Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/192535. Review of applications will begin October 1st and will close November 10th. Applicants should submit the following materials: Cover Letter : A one to two-page document articulating how your qualifications and experience align with the position, and how you will contribute to the University of Notre Dame’s mission and the Department of Biological Sciences. Curriculum Vitae (CV) . Research Statement: A two-page document excluding references summarizing past accomplishments, future research directions, and plans for securing external funding. Teaching & Mentorship Statement: A one-page document detailing teaching philosophy, experience, and plans for mentoring a diverse student body. Contact Information for Three Referees: A list of three references. For questions regarding the position, please contact the search chair Jeff Schorey, Professor, Department of Biological Sciences ( schorey.1@nd.edu )."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192740": {
      "title": "Open Rank Position in Painting & Drawing",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: Art, Art History and Design Location: Notre Dame, IN The Department of Art, Art History and Design at the University of Notre Dame invites applications for an open-rank tenure-track faculty position (Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor) in Painting & Drawing to begin fall 2027. The Department seeks an innovative artist with a broad understanding of contemporary practice in painting and drawing. Practitioners engaged with digital, installation, video, or similar expanded frameworks of painting and drawing methodology are especially encouraged to apply. Candidates should be knowledgeable in contemporary technical and critical aspects of the mediums and highly committed to teaching undergraduate and graduate courses related to painting and drawing in a multidisciplinary curriculum. Qualified applicants must have an active exhibition record that demonstrates a trajectory of increasing recognition required of a research university. The appointed faculty member will be expected to contribute to curriculum development, teach courses, engage in academic and professional service, participate in departmental critiques and MFA theses, and work collaboratively across all three divisions of the department. The terminal degree (MFA) in studio art is required for this position. Candidates must have a minimum of three years of teaching and professional experience beyond graduate school. The Department of Art, Art History and Design has 22 full-time faculty members and is located within the College of Arts and Letters. The studio art program is one part of an exciting, trans-disciplinary creative studies department including studio artists, art historians, and visual communication and industrial designers. The new Raclin-Murphy Art Museum and the Charles B. Hayes Sculpture Park are located on campus. The department is committed to a collaborative teaching and learning environment within the context of a liberal arts education. https://artdept.nd.edu/ To Apply: Please include the following as one or more PDF files: 1) a letter of interest outlining how your credentials and experience meet the qualifications and responsibilities of the position, 2) curriculum vitae, 3) relevant syllabi, 4) a visual portfolio including evidence of professional practice/ links to professional websites are also welcome, 5) three references with contact information only."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192847": {
      "title": "Faculty in Visual Communication Design",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: Art, Art History and Design Location: Notre Dame, IN The Department of Art, Art History, and Design at the University of Notre Dame invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor for an accomplished designer whose research integrates critical scholarly inquiry in Visual Communication Design. We seek a candidate whose expertise demonstrates a strong design foundation in both traditional and emerging areas of the discipline, with particular emphasis on forward-thinking approaches that prepare students for the rapidly evolving landscape of the 21st century. Duties include research, teaching, and service. Candidates should demonstrate a deep understanding of contemporary design scholarship, technological innovation, and critical methodologies that reflect the expanding role of design in society. The successful applicant will contribute to the development of a dynamic, inclusive, and relevant curriculum at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, fostering interdisciplinary collaboration, creative problem-solving, and socially engaged design practices while successfully preparing them to thrive in diverse design careers. This position offers the opportunity to join a vibrant academic community committed to advancing the role of design in shaping culture, technology, and the human experience. About the Design Program Visual Communication Design at the University of Notre Dame lays emphasis on the art, science, and practice of building platforms and experiences that create tangible communication between content and users through traditional and contemporary media. The VCD program offers instruction in areas of typography, studio practice, brand identity, packaging, UX/UI, motion design, interaction design, community-based design, information design, and design research. The VCD program is established around human-centered design principles. The program focuses on building a deep empathy with stakeholders and solving wicked problems with contextual solutions that derive from a holistic vision. The design division (ID and Visual Communication Design) offers MFA, BFA, and BA degrees, as well as a minor. The Minor is a dynamic catalyst for process-based, cross-disciplinary collaboration between various academic departments across the university. Design is part of a multi-disciplinary creative studies department that includes studio artists and art historians, all committed to a collaborative teaching and learning environment within the context of a liberal arts education. Minimum Qualifications ● A terminal degree (MFA, MDes) or equivalent in Visual Communication Design, Graphic Design, Interaction Design, or a closely related field. ● Candidates are expected to demonstrate disciplinary expertise and excellence with a strong record of accomplishment in the field of visual communication design. ● Candidates must demonstrate a viable, socially-engaged, and intellectually rigorous research trajectory, which can be situated across diverse areas, including interdisciplinary scholarship that bridges critical inquiry with creative investigation. ● To support instruction across the curriculum and contribute to curricular development, candidates should hold expertise in two or more of the following areas: motion graphics, UI/UX, digital/emerging/social media and technologies, design history, speculative or critical design, art direction/advertising design, brand/identity, package design, typography, and data visualization. ● Demonstrated proficiency in discipline-standard technologies, software, and contemporary digital media platforms. Preferred Qualifications • Evidence of effective teaching in higher education. • A record of integrating culturally responsive and/or environmentally sustainable perspectives into teaching or research. • Demonstrated versatility across both traditional and digital media. • Experience incorporating emerging technologies into pedagogy or creative endeavors. • Strong interpersonal and collaborative skills, with the ability to contribute effectively to a diverse academic community. To Apply: Please include the following: » A letter of interest » Curriculum vitae » Statement of research interests and/or professional practice agenda » Statement of teaching philosophy » 20 examples of research/creative work and/or professional practice » Examples of student work (if available) » Three references (contact information only) Links to professional websites are welcome. This institution is using Interfolio's By Committee to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. For help signing up, accessing your account, or submitting your application, please check out our help and support section or get in touch via email at help@interfolio.com or phone at (877) 997-8807. Deadline Review of candidates will begin December 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192926": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Faculty Position (Open Rank) - Robotics",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Engineering: Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering Location: Notre Dame Main Campus The Department of Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering at the University of Notre Dame invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in robotics. This search is part of sustained growth in robotics at Notre Dame. Recent investments include a cross-departmental effort in Physical AI and Robotics that connects Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering with Computer Science and Engineering and with Electrical Engineering. Robotics at Notre Dame is organized around a distinctive identity in human-centered robotics, built on established strengths in mechanics, modeling, control, and experimentally grounded robotic systems. While applications are welcome from all areas of robotics, the Department is especially interested in candidates working in one or more of the following areas: ●       Sensing, actuation, and robotic materials, including soft, multifunctional, and biohybrid systems ●       Dexterous manipulation and physical interaction with complex or unstructured environments ●       Wearable, assistive, and rehabilitation robotics, with particular interest in upper limbs ●       Robotic mobility for terrestrial, aerial, and aquatic systems operating autonomously in extreme environments ●       Integrated design, modeling, control, and estimation for emerging robotic systems Across these areas, the Department is particularly interested in candidates who advance robot learning and data-driven reasoning in ways that are grounded in the physics, mechanics, sensing, and control of real robotic systems and demonstrated through meaningful experimental deployment. The successful candidate will join an interconnected robotics community with active collaborations across engineering, computing, the health sciences, and industry, and with shared experimental facilities and testbeds that support work at the intersection of hardware, control, and learning. Additional information about robotics at Notre Dame is available at robotics.nd.edu. The Department seeks to attract, develop, and retain exceptional faculty members with strong records of achievement and significant future potential. The search is primarily aimed at the assistant professor level; however, candidates at all ranks with strong records and promise will be considered, and possible position titles include Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor. Candidates at the entry level must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D. degree, or have completed all but the dissertation, in Aerospace Engineering, Mechanical Engineering, Robotics, or a closely related field; candidates at the advanced assistant, associate, or full professor level must hold a Ph.D. in one of these fields. Duties for a successful candidate will include teaching, research and service. Successful candidates will demonstrate the potential to achieve an internationally distinguished record of scholarship, establish an externally funded research program, excel in undergraduate and graduate education, and contribute actively to their research community and to the University. Applicants are invited to provide any or all of the following: a cover letter; a curriculum vitae; a comprehensive research plan; a statement of teaching philosophy and interests; contact information for three professional references; and a statement regarding potential engagement with and commitment to the mission of the University of Notre Dame. All materials should be submitted via the application website. To guarantee full consideration, applications must be received by November 1, 2026; however, applications will be accepted until December 1, 2026. Questions regarding this position may be directed to the search chair, Professor Tengfei Luo (tluo@nd.edu)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192927": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Faculty Position (Open Rank) - all areas",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Engineering: Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering Location: Notre Dame Main campus The Department of Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering at the University of Notre Dame invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position. Applications are welcome from all areas of aerospace and mechanical engineering. The Department is particularly interested in candidates whose work complements and bridges existing strengths in its five research pillars: bioengineering; computational engineering; fluid mechanics; materials, energy, and manufacturing; and robotics and controls. Strategic priorities include human health and well-being; sustainable energy, propulsion, and aerospace systems; advanced materials and manufacturing; dynamics, control, and automation; physical artificial intelligence and autonomy; and research that couples AI with physics-based modeling, simulation, and experimentation. The Department seeks to attract, develop, and retain exceptional faculty members with strong records of achievement and significant future potential. The search is primarily aimed at the assistant professor level; however, candidates at all ranks with strong records and promise will be considered, and possible position titles include Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor. Candidates at the entry level must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D. degree, or have completed all but the dissertation, in Aerospace Engineering, Mechanical Engineering, or a closely related field; candidates at the advanced assistant, associate, or full professor level must hold a Ph.D. in one of these fields. Duties for a successful candidate will include teaching, research and service. Successful candidates will demonstrate the potential to achieve an internationally distinguished record of scholarship, establish an externally funded research program, excel in undergraduate and graduate education, and contribute actively to their research community and to the University. Notre Dame is a private research university with a distinctive mission that joins ambitious scholarship to a commitment to the common good. New faculty join a collegial department with extensive shared experimental and computational facilities, competitive start-up support, and well-established collaborations across the College of Engineering and with university-wide institutes and initiatives in energy, health, data and computing, and artificial intelligence. The Department is concurrently conducting a separate search for a tenure-track faculty position in robotics. Candidates whose interests span both searches are welcome to apply to either; applications will be considered by both committees as appropriate. Candidates at the entry level must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D. degree, or have completed all but the dissertation, in Aerospace Engineering, Mechanical Engineering, or a closely related field; candidates at the advanced assistant, associate, or full professor level must hold a Ph.D. in one of these fields. Applicants are invited to provide any or all of the following: a cover letter; a curriculum vitae; a comprehensive research plan; a statement of teaching philosophy and interests; contact information for three professional references; and a statement regarding potential engagement with and commitment to the mission of the University of Notre Dame. All materials should be submitted via the application website . To guarantee full consideration, applications must be received by November 1, 2026; however, applications will be accepted until December 1, 2026. Questions regarding this position may be directed to the search chair, Professor Tengfei Luo (tluo@nd.edu)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192967": {
      "title": "Early Modern British Literature (tenure-track)",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: English  Location: Notre Dame, IN  USA The Department of English at the University of Notre Dame invites applications for a full-time, tenure‐track Assistant Professor position in Early Modern British Literature. Position duties include teaching, research, and service. We welcome candidates who think in fresh and innovative ways about period, genres, and forms. Candidates with research interests in the seventeenth century are especially encouraged to apply. Candidates at the entry level must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D.  degree or have completed all but dissertation in Early Modern/Renaissance Literature. Candidates at the advanced assistant level must have a Ph.D. in Early Modern/Renaissance Literature. To apply, we ask candidates to submit: Application letter Current CV Informal inquiries and nominations should be directed to: Professor Laura Knoppers, Chair of the Search Committee at lknoppe1@nd.edu Applications must be received by Monday, October 26, for full consideration. We expect to conduct all interviews via Zoom. Candidates may be asked to provide additional materials later in the review process, including a writing sample and a dossier of references. Please do not send any additional materials unless requested. Finalists will be invited to visit campus for departmental interviews."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192973": {
      "title": "Victorian Literature (Assistant Professor, tenure-track)",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: English  Location: Notre Dame, IN  USA The Department of English at the University of Notre Dame invites applications for a full-time, tenure‐track appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor in Victorian Literature. Candidates should show evidence of an ambitious program of research and a commitment to excellence in teaching and service. We welcome applicants working in any genre and with any critical approach. The position requires teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels (normally two courses per semester), including introductory or survey courses in Victorian literature, and courses in more specialized topics. The successful candidate will also mentor and advise students at all levels. The anticipated start date for the position is August 2007. Candidates at the entry level must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D. degree or have completed all but dissertation in Victorian Literature or a related field with degree expected by August 1, 2027. Candidates at the advanced assistant level must have a Ph.D. in Victorian Literature or a related field. To apply, candidates should submit the following: Application letter Current CV The names and contact information of three referees (who will be contacted if the candidate is called to interview) Candidates may be asked to provide additional materials later in the review process, including a writing sample and dossier of references. Applications must be received by October 22, 2026 , for full consideration. We expect to conduct all interviews of longlisted candidates over Zoom. Finalists will be invited to visit campus for departmental interviews. Inquiries may be directed to Professor Yasmin Solomonescu, Chair of the Search Committee, at ysolomon@nd.edu . Further information about the Department of English and the University of Notre Dame can be found at https://english.nd.edu/ and https://www.nd.edu/ ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193155": {
      "title": "Victorian Literature (Associate or Full Professor)",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: English  Location: Notre Dame, IN  USA The Department of English at the University of Notre Dame invites applications for a full-time appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor in Victorian Literature. Candidates should have a distinguished record of publication and a demonstrated commitment to excellence in teaching and service. We welcome applicants working in any genre and with any critical approach. The position requires teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels (normally two courses per semester), including introductory or survey courses in Victorian literature, and courses in more specialized topics. The successful candidate will also mentor and advise students at all levels. We are particularly seeking candidates eager to take a leading role in the life of our department and university, and in the profession as a whole. A Ph.D. in Victorian Literature or a related field is required. The anticipated start date for the position is August 2027. To apply, candidates should submit the following: Application letter Current CV The names and contact information of three referees (who will be contacted if the candidate is called to interview) Candidates may be asked to provide additional materials later in the review process, including a writing sample and dossier of references. Applications must be received by October 22, 2026 , for full consideration. We expect to conduct all interviews of longlisted candidates over Zoom. Finalists will be invited to visit campus for departmental interviews. Inquiries may be directed to Professor Yasmin Solomonescu, Chair of the Search Committee, at ysolomon@nd.edu . Further information about the Department of English and the University of Notre Dame can be found at https://english.nd.edu/ and https://www.nd.edu/ ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193341": {
      "title": "Open-Rank Tenure-Track Faculty Position: IT, Analytics, and Operations with a focus on Data, AI, and Computing",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: Mendoza College of Business: Information Technology, Analytics, and Operations Location: Notre Dame, IN The IT, Analytics, and Operations (ITAO) Department, at the University of Notre Dame’s Mendoza College of Business, in collaboration with the Data, AI, and Computing Initiative (DAC) , announce s an open-rank position at the assistant, associate, or full professor level. The ideal candidate has a strong record of interesting and impactful scholarship or great potential for such scholarship. This entails significant publication success at premier outlets and/or a strong pipeline of innovative and rigorous research relevant to Data, AI, and Computing in business applications. Preference will be given to individuals whose research complements the interests of our existing faculty. Candidates pushing the envelope in exciting boundary-spanning areas at the intersection of business, analytical modeling, computational social science, machine learning, network science, human-centered artificial intelligence, or experimentation methods are encouraged to apply. The ITAO Department is home to a host of approaches and backgrounds, making for a vibrant research environment, which results in frequent, compelling, and unique collaborative research projects among our faculty. The department also houses the Human-centered Analytics Lab (HAL), Generative AI, Management, and Accountability Lab (GAMA), and the Humanitarian Operations Lab (HOPE). ITAO is well-aligned with the university's Data, AI, and Computing Initiative, which launched in September 2025. The initiative’s aim is to advance purposeful data, AI, and computing—excelling in foundational research while catalyzing interdisciplinary collaboration and real-world translation to address pressing societal challenges. Our ideal candidate will have demonstrated success in the classroom with an interest in teaching in areas related to analytics, broadly defined. In addition to our PhD program in Analytics, the ITAO Department offers an undergraduate major in Business Analytics, two AI minors, and a Master of Science in Business Analytics (MSBA) program. The Mendoza College of Business is home to a highly regarded Masters of Business Administration (MBA) program, for which we offer a major in Analytics & AI. The ITAO Department is an integral part of the University of Notre Dame’s interdisciplinary efforts to make an impact on scholarship and society through analytical modeling, operations research, data analytics, artificial intelligence, and computationally intensive research.  There are also interdisciplinary collaborative opportunities, such as the possibility of affiliate faculty status in other departments (e.g., CS or Statistics) or the Lucy Family Institute for Data and Society. ITAO researchers enjoy generous allocations of resources from Notre Dame’s Center for Research Computing and the support of a staff of three full-time Mendoza College of Business data scientists and a director of behavioral research who oversees the Mendoza Behavioral Lab. Duties include research, teaching, and service. The teaching load is 9 credit hours per year and will also contribute to courses supporting our AI minors. Salary and research budget are highly competitive, with generous summer support based on research productivity. Successful candidates would, depending on qualifications and impact, be hired into a tenure-track assistant professor, tenured associate professor, or full professor role. Candidates at the entry level must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D. degree or have completed all but dissertation in Analytics or a related field with degree expected by August 2027. Candidates at the advanced assistant, associate, or full professor level must have a Ph.D. in Analytics or a related field. Applicants should submit a CV and a cover letter describing the applicant’s interest in the position and one document with three references along with their contact information.  The search committee intends to review applications on a rolling basis. Applications will be accepted until positions are filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193390": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Tenure-track, in Theology and Technology",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: Theology Location: Notre Dame, IN The Department of Theology at the University of Notre Dame in Notre Dame, Indiana, invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track professorship at the assistant professor rank in the field of Theology and Technology. The department is particularly interested in candidates who study the ethics of digital technologies. Successful candidates will demonstrate not only expertise in the applied ethics of technology but also grounding in broader theological sources. The starting date for this position will be August 23, 2027. Responsibilities will include research and publication, teaching and mentoring students at all levels—doctoral (and pre-doctoral), ministerial, and undergraduate—and service to the department and university. Information about Notre Dame is available at https://www.nd.edu/ , the Department of Theology at https://theology.nd.edu/ , and the University mission statement at https://www.nd.edu/about/mission/ . The successful candidates will show evidence of strong scholarship and a trajectory toward a field-leading contribution to the study of theology and technology. They will also be sympathetic to the mission of the University of Notre Dame, which seeks a distinctive educational and scholarly excellence that is founded on its Catholic identity. Candidates at the entry level must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D. degree or have completed all but dissertation in moral theology or a closely related field with successful dissertation defense by August 1, 2027. Candidates at the advanced level must have a Ph.D. in moral theology or a closely related field. Please submit a letter of application, current curriculum vitae, one writing sample, and three letters of recommendation. The review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026, with applications being accepted through October 31, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193402": {
      "title": "Associate/Full Professor: Science, Mathematics, Engineering, Computer Science or Integrated STEM Education",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: Office of the Provost: Institute for Educational Initiatives Location: University of Notre Dame The Institute for Educational Initiatives (IEI or The Institute) at the University of Notre Dame invites applications for an Associate or Full Professor in STEM education (or one of its disciplinary derivatives) to serve as a senior faculty member of the IEI and provide strategic leadership as the director of the Notre Dame Center for STEM Education . We seek an accomplished scholar and collaborative academic leader with a strong record of research, external funding, mentorship, and professional service. Candidates should be committed to advancing rigorous and impactful educational research and creating bridges between research and our existing strong programmatic work that improves educational opportunities and outcomes for all students. The faculty role is a full-time, 9-month appointment with an anticipated start date of July 1, 2027. The concurrent secondary appointment as director of the Notre Dame Center for STEM Education is a 12-month appointment also beginning July 1, 2027. Research and Leadership Responsibilities The successful candidate will maintain a nationally recognized research agenda focused on STEM education while also providing strategic vision and leadership for the Center for STEM Education.  The Institute seeks a scholar whose work demonstrates methodological rigor, interdisciplinary collaboration, and meaningful educational impact. The faculty member will be expected to sustain an active research program that advances understanding related to areas like STEM learning, teaching, educational systems, or educator development.  The successful candidate should demonstrate a commitment to bridging research and practice and developing partnerships that improve educational opportunities for students and educators. In addition to maintaining an active scholarly agenda, the faculty member will provide leadership for the Center for STEM Education by supporting strategic planning, fostering a vibrant intellectual community among faculty, staff, students, and research partners, and collaborating with existing program directors to advance the mission and impact of its two anchor programs: ND PACT and the Notre Dame STEM Teaching Fellows. The position will also include mentoring responsibilites, particularly in supporting junior faculty, postdoctoral scholars, and research staff in the areas such as research design, publication development, grant writing and professional growth.  The successful candidate will contribute to building a collaborative and ambitious culture of scholarship across the Institute. The Center was founded on the belief that research and participation in STEM education is not a neutral pursuit, but rather a means for promoting the innate dignity of the human person and fostering the common good. Members of the Center work at the intersection of inquiry and practice, in which rigorous research informs our professional formation efforts with educators and in which our deep engagement with teachers’ classrooms and school communities equips our researchers to explore relevant issues that advance the broader field while reflecting Notre Dame’s broader commitment to advancing human flourishing and the common good. Thus, this position is designed for a scholar whose work bridges educational research, practice, and institutional leadership and who can contribute meaningfully to the intellectual and strategic growth of the Center and the IEI. Teaching Responsibilities The faculty member will teach courses at the undergraduate level (as part of our Education, Schooling, and Society minor/supplementary major) and/or graduate levels (e.g. with the ACE Teaching Fellows program) within the Institute for Educational Initiatives. Teaching responsibilities may include courses related to STEM education, educational research methodologies, learning sciences, improvement science, educational innovation, or other areas aligned with the faculty member’s expertise and the needs of Institute programs. The successful candidate will also mentor undergraduate and graduate students through research opportunities, capstone projects, theses, and collaborative field-based work. Given the Institute’s broad portfolio of degree-granting and professional formation programs, the faculty member may contribute to multiple programs across the IEI. Qualifications The position is open to any applicant with an earned doctorate (Ph.D., Ed.D., or equivalent) in a STEM discipline or a related discipline relevant to STEM education. Applications should include: 1) a cover letter indicating the candidate’s interest in this position at the University of Notre Dame; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) teaching evaluations (if available); 4) Additional documentation, including letters of recommendation, may also be requested"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193414": {
      "title": "Faculty Position in Innovative Construction Engineering and Infrastructure Systems",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Engineering: Civil & Environmental Engineering & Earth Sciences Location: University of Notre Dame The Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering and Earth Sciences (CEEES) at the University of Notre Dame is expanding its research and educational strengths in Construction Engineering and Management and invites applications for multiple open rank faculty positions in Innovative Construction Engineering and Infrastructure Systems . Appointments may be made at the Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor level. Areas of particular interest include, but are not restricted to: Intelligent, Autonomous, and Geospatial Construction Systems: construction robotics and autonomous equipment; geomatics, sensing, reality capture, and computer vision; digital twins and cyber-physical systems; human-robot collaboration; and AI-enabled construction planning and operations. Future Construction and Infrastructure Systems: advanced and automated construction methods; industrialized, off-site, and additive construction; construction production, productivity, and sustainability; workforce transformation; safety and human factors; and human-AI collaboration. Construction and Infrastructure in Complex Environments: innovative approaches to constructing, maintaining, and adapting infrastructure in dense urban, constrained, remote, extreme, and extraterrestrial environments, including challenges associated with existing infrastructure, limited access and resources, and complex project settings. These areas are intended to be illustrative rather than restrictive, and candidates pursuing innovative research that broadly advances construction engineering and infrastructure systems are encouraged to apply. Successful candidates are expected to develop internationally recognized and externally funded research programs, teach undergraduate and graduate courses in construction engineering and management, mentor students, and contribute to collaborative research within the Department, across the University, and with industry and government partners. Candidates at the entry level must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D. degree or have completed all but dissertation in Civil Engineering, Construction Engineering and Management, or a closely related field. Candidates at the advanced level must have a Ph.D. in Civil Engineering, Construction Engineering and Management, or a closely related field. Applicants are invited to provide any or all of the following: cover letter; curriculum vitae; comprehensive research plan; statement of teaching philosophy and interests; contact information for three professional references; and statement regarding potential engagement with and commitment to the mission of the University of Notre Dame. To guarantee full consideration, applications must be received by November 15, 2026; however, applications will be accepted until December 1, 2026 . Interested candidates should submit their complete application materials through the online portal at https://apply.interfolio.com/193414 . For any inquiries or questions related to this search, please feel free to contact Dr. Wenying Ji, Chair of the Faculty Search Committee, at wji@nd.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193534": {
      "title": "McMeel Family Chair in Shakespeare Studies (Endowed Professor, Tenured)",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: English  Location: Notre Dame, IN  USA The Departments of English and Film, Television and Theatre at the University of Notre Dame invite applications for the McMeel Family Chair in Shakespeare Studies. We seek a widely recognized leader in the field with a distinguished record of research and publication and a demonstrated commitment to excellence in teaching. Duties will include teaching, research, and service. The successful candidate will be appointed at the rank of full professor with tenure with a start date in August 2027. Review of applications will begin November 1 and continue until the search is closed. A Ph.D. (or equivalent degree) in English or Film, Television, and Theatre or a related field is required. Must be a tenured faculty member (or equivalent international rank) at current institution. To apply, candidates should submit the following through Interfolio: An application letter A current CV The committee will conduct initial interviews via Zoom. Finalists will be invited to campus for interviews. Informal inquiries and nominations should be directed to Prof. Jesse Lander at jlander@nd.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194213": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Teaching Professor in Italian Language and Culture, University of Notre Dame",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: Romance Languages & Literatures Location: Notre Dame, IN The Department of Romance Languages and Literatures seeks to appoint a Teaching Professor in Italian language and culture to begin in August 2027. The ideal candidate will be able to teach at all levels of the curriculum, from elementary language and culture through advanced courses, and will contribute to our dynamic undergraduate program committed to innovation and teaching excellence. This is a non-tenure track, renewable, full-time, and benefits-eligible position. The appointment is for a 3/3 teaching load with the expectation of service. Appointment to the rank of Assistant or Associate Teaching Professor will depend on experience and qualifications. Notre Dame offers competitive salary and benefits, research support, and excellent opportunities for professional development; the Department of Romance Languages and Literatures has been at the forefront in the design and implementation of pioneering strategies to support foreign language education at Notre Dame. Therefore, the ideal candidate should demonstrate excellence in teaching through innovative methodologies and new technologies in the language classroom, as well as an interest in teaching language courses at all levels. Native or near-native proficiency in Italian and English is essential, as is substantial experience teaching Italian courses at the university level. PhD in Italian or a related field is required. For consideration, please send through Interfolio a letter of application; full curriculum vitae; teaching evaluations for multiple courses; statement of teaching philosophy; and three letters of recommendation that address teaching experience by November 9 th , 2026.  The search committee will begin reviewing applications immediately after and will continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194243": {
      "title": "AY 26/27 Assistant Research Professor",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: Psychology Location: University of Notre Dame The Department of Psychology at the University of Notre Dame invites applications for an assistant research professor specializing in fMRI-based studies, network neuroscience, and the application of statistical machine learning and AI approaches to neuroimaging data.  The position will begin in the Fall of 2027. Research (80%): Research responsibilities include all aspects of the research process, with a particular emphasis on fMRI-based studies, network neuroscience, and the application of statistical machine learning and AI approaches to neuroimaging data. This includes developing research/project ideas, advanced fMRI data analysis and network modeling, integrating machine learning/AI methods for predictive modeling and large-scale data analysis, writing grants for external funding (with a preference for projects leveraging neuroimaging, network neuroscience, and computational approaches), developing study designs and project protocols, developing and maintaining IRB protocols, overseeing all aspects of study implementation for ongoing externally funded projects, managing budgets, preparing annual reports to funders, and contributing to dissemination of findings by authoring/co-authoring conference presentations and publications. Teaching/Mentorship (10%): Responsibilities include weekly meetings with graduate students in the Decision Neuroscience Lab (DNL; https://www.decisionneurosciencelab.org), as needed meetings with individual undergraduate research assistants for feedback, advanced training and guidance, weekly meetings with undergraduate RAs in the DNL lab meetings, mentoring undergraduates pursuing independent projects and honors theses, serving on masters and doctoral committees, and assisting with developing and organizing course content for courses like Cognitive Neuroscience Methods (grad and undergrad versions). The position will involve mentoring students in fMRI analysis pipelines, network neuroscience methods, and computational/statistical approaches including machine learning and AI. Administrative (5%): Administrative responsibilities revolve primarily around the DNL, including recruiting, hiring, training and managing staff, managing day-to-day aspects of operating the lab and developing and maintaining community connections/networks in the greater South Bend area. Service (5%): Service responsibilities as assigned by the Department. Service to the field includes regular ad-hoc reviews, participating as a reviewer and panel chair for various external grant reviews, and related work. The position is for a limited term and is contingent upon continued availability of funding. Ph.D. in Psychology or related field Candidates at the entry level must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D. degree or have completed all but dissertation in Psychology or a closely related field. Candidates at the advanced assistant professor level must have a Ph.D. in Psychology or a closely related field. To guarantee full consideration, applications must be received by [December 15, 2026], but the review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Required application materials include a CV; statement on research; and 3 letters of recommendation. Questions and informal inquiries regarding this position may be directed to the chair of the search committee: Aron Barbey (abarbey@nd.edu)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194462": {
      "title": "University Chair Full Professorship in Systematic Theology",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: Theology Location: Notre Dame, IN The Department of Theology at the University of Notre Dame in Notre Dame, Indiana, invites applications for an endowed Chair professorship, appointed at the rank of full professor, in Catholic systematic theology. All subspecialities within systematic theology are welcome to apply, though applicants should make clear their methodological approaches, subspecialty expertise(s), and how both might contribute to Notre Dame systematic theology’s mission of scholarship “in the service of ‘faith seeking understanding,” exploring “the meaning, interrelatedness, and claims to the truth of the Christian tradition’s basic expressions of faith.”  The starting date for this position will be August 23 , 2027. Responsibilities will include research and publication; teaching and mentoring students at all levels (doctoral and pre-doctoral, ministerial, and undergraduate); and service to the department and university. Information about Notre Dame is available at https://www.nd.edu/ , the Department of Theology at https://theology.nd.edu/ , and the University mission statement at https://www.nd.edu/about/mission/ . The successful candidate will show evidence of a field-leading scholarship in their field of expertise, as well as a capacity to engage the intellectual mission of the department as a whole. They will also be sympathetic to the mission of the University of Notre Dame, which seeks a distinctive educational and scholarly excellence that is founded on its Catholic identity. Candidates must have a Ph.D. in Theology or a closely related field. Please submit a letter of application and current curriculum vitae. Applications will be accepted on a rolling basis but full consideration is assured if applications are received by October 23, 2026. Given the senior status of the applicants invited, all applications will be reviewed in strict confidence, and applicants will be contacted individually prior to any campus invitation.   Confidential inquiries can be addressed to Fr. Kevin Grove ( kgrove1@nd.edu ) or Dr. Jennifer Newsome Martin ( jmarti16@nd.edu )."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195572": {
      "title": "Director, Kroc Institute for International Peace Studies and Associate/Full Professor",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: Keough School of Global Affairs Location: University of Notre Dame campus The University of Notre Dame (Notre Dame) and the Keough School of Global Affairs are accepting applications for a full-time, tenured position at the associate or full professor level. The ideal candidate will be an accomplished scholar, strategic leader, and culture builder that will also serve as the next Director of the Kroc Institute for International Peace Studies. The Director will lead a globally recognized institute composed of a committed group of research scholars and practitioners in charting a compelling vision for the future. This is an important leadership role that is central to the mission of a premier global Catholic research university. The Director role offers an opportunity to make an outsized impact in realizing Kroc’s mission to study the causes of violent conflict and strategies for a just and sustainable peace. The ideal candidate will be a distinguished scholar whose research and policy engagement focus on issues relevant to the study or implementation of peace. Candidates should have a Ph.D. and rigorous training in law, history, peace studies, political science, sociology, theology, or a related field, with a record of significant intellectual contributions and academic achievement to receive tenure at Notre Dame. This is a full-time, tenured appointment, with administrative responsibilities and teaching duties at the undergraduate and graduate levels, ideally to begin in August 2027. The Kroc Institute was founded in 1986 through the shared vision of long-time Notre Dame President Rev. Theodore M. Hesburgh, C.S.C., and philanthropist Joan B. Kroc. Inspired by a commitment to peace, human rights, and the reduction of conflict, the Institute has grown into one of the world’s leading centers for peace studies research, education, and practice. The Institute brings together scholars from multiple fields to address some of the world’s most pressing challenges. Research areas span peace processes, international mediation, conflict resolution, transitional justice, environmental justice, nuclear disarmament, human rights, migration, religion and peacebuilding, genocide prevention, and emerging issues such as technology and polarization. In 2014, the Institute became a founding unit of Notre Dame’s Keough School of Global Affairs. A defining characteristic of the Kroc Institute is its commitment to bridging scholarship and practice; producing interdisciplinary research that not only advances knowledge but also informs policy and action. Faculty and programs engage directly with governments, international organizations, nongovernmental organizations, and local communities around the world. Initiatives such as the Peace Accords Matrix and the Catholic Peacebuilding Network are significant and renowned initiatives to translate scholarship into practical impact. Its academic portfolio includes programs at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Supported by a substantial endowment, and a global alumni network of more than 2,000, the Institute occupies a uniquely influential position within the field. The Director of the Kroc Institute reports directly to the Dean of the Keough School of Global Affairs. The next Director will be charged with developing an innovative intellectual agenda; continue to integrate with the Keough School; enhance Kroc’s leadership in peace studies; and foster a collaborative and inclusive community. The Director will have an opportunity to build upon the Institute’s remarkable 40 year legacy while shaping a new future as the leading center for peace studies grounded in Notre Dame’s identity as a Catholic research institution and its enduring commitment to human dignity, justice, and the common good. The University of Notre Dame has retained Isaacson, Miller to assist with this recruitment. Instructions for applying, nominating, and/or inquiring about the position can be found at https://www.imsearch.com/open-searches/university-notre-dame-keough-school-global-affairs/director-kroc-institute . Applicants must apply through Isaacson, Miller to be considered for the position."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190407": {
      "title": "History of Global Catholicism (open rank)",
      "description": "Department: University of Notre Dame: College of Arts & Letters: History Location: Notre Dame, Indiana The History Department at the University of Notre Dame invites applications for an open-rank tenure-track faculty position (assistant professor, associate professor, or full professor) in the History of Global Catholicism, understood broadly. We invite applications from scholars who study Catholicism as a global and dynamic tradition, attending to its circulation beyond and across national frameworks, and to the ways local histories and cultures have shaped and reshaped this multicultural and multilingual institution of 1.4 billion adherents. We will also consider applications from scholars who study the impact of Catholic people, institutions, beliefs, rituals, and practices on broader global phenomena, especially but not only in the global South, including but not limited to colonization/decolonization; migration; intellectual life; social structures and social policies; gender, enslavement; international diplomacy, and more. Position duties include research, teaching, and service. The successful candidate will be prepared to contribute to and engage with Notre Dame’s Global Catholic Research Initiative , launched in 2025. Candidates at the entry level must have completed all requirements for a PhD degree or have completed all but dissertation in the History of Global Catholicism or a closely related field. Candidates at the advanced assistant, associate, or full professor level must have a PhD in the History of Global Catholicism or a closely related field. Application materials (a curriculum vitae and letter of interest, including the names of three people who could be asked for letters of reference) should be submitted through Interfolio no later than October 1, 2026 . Nominations and inquiries may be sent via email to: Edward (Ted) Beatty, chair of the search committee ( ebeatty@nd.edu ). Information about Notre Dame is available here , and about the department at history.nd.edu ."
    }
  },
